1 OCCU 17th Chief of Clan MacLean, 1st Baronet of Morvaren
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Lachlan Maclean,17th Chief of Clan MacLean, 1st Baronet of Morven was the son of Hector 'Og' Maclean, 11th of Duart and Morvern and Jennette Mackenzie. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Mary Macleod, daughter of Sir Roderick Macleod, 15th of MacLeod and Isabella Macdonald. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Tarbert Sheriffdom [Scotland] between 1628 and 1633. He was created 1st Baronet Maclean, of Morven, co. Argyll [Nova Scotia] on 3 September 1631, with special remainder to his 
2 CONC heirs male whatsoever.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a Royalist during the Civil War. A key ally of James Graham, 1st Marquess of Montrose. Montrose was a famous Royalist general. Lachlan and his clan fought alongside Montrose in several important battles.
2 CONT
2 CONT One famous event happened the night before the Battle of Inverlochy. Lachlan met with Montrose in a place called Lochaber. He joined the battle with about 30 of his men.
2 CONT
2 CONT He gathered his whole clan after that battle. They joined Montrose again after the Battle of Alford and stayed with him through the Battle of Kilsyth. These were all major victories for the Royalists.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Battle of Kilsyth, Lachlan and another brave leader, Alasdair MacColla, won a surprising victory. They defeated a much larger group of their enemies, even though they had fewer men.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lachlan was preparing to join Montrose for another fight. However, he learned that the King had ordered Montrose to stop fighting. So, Lachlan returned home and stayed there quietly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, a general named Sir David Leslie came to the Island of Mull. He had many soldiers and forced Lachlan to hand over eight Irish gentlemen who were seeking safety with him. Seven of these men were sadly executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Lachlan Maclean was married to Mary MacLeod, the second daughter of Sir Roderick Macleod of Macleod, 15th Chief, by whom he had two sons and three daughters: 
2 CONT
2 CONT #Isobel Maclean (circa 1630-?), who married Sir Ewen Cameron of Lochiel (1629–1719)[5]
2 CONT #Mary Maclean, who married Sir Lachlan "Mor" Mackinnon (28th clan chief)
2 CONT #Marian Maclean, who died young and unmarried
2 CONT #Sir Hector Maclean, 2nd Baronet (c1640-1651), his heir and successor
2 CONT #Sir Allan Maclean, 3rd Baronet (1645–1674)
2 CONT
2 CONT He had ruled for twenty three years before his own death in 1649. He was succeeded by his eldest son, Sir Hector Maclean, 2nd Baronet.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
