1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Anna Elisabeth Von Rohr (1701-1744) Örbom (b. January 22, 1700/01, Sweden, Carelia, Kexholm - d. 07.09.1767 Naantali/Nådental, Egentliga Finland, Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestry: She was a descendent of Hans von Rohr (1484-1569) Lord of Freienstien, Meyenburg, Neuhausen, Dremnitz and Leddin 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: Her father was Joachim Von Rohr (1677-1757), Lieutenant Colonel and Commander of Dalarö Garrison, the military fortress east of Stockholm, on the Anna Elisabeth von Rohr (1701-1767) Örbom (b. January 22, 1700/01, Sweden - d
2 CONC . September 07, 1767, Naantali/Nådental, Egentliga Finland, Sweden) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestry: She was a descendant of Hans von Rohr (1484-1569) Lord of Freienstien, Meyenburg, Neuhausen, Dremnitz and Leddin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: Her father was Joachim Von Rohr (1677-1757), Lieutenant Colonel and Commander of Dalarö Garrison, the military fortress east of Stockholm, on the Baltic. Her mother was Catharina Charlotta Klingenberg (1680-1758). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Prisoner of war: She was taken prisoner along with her father at the Siege of Viborg in 1710. All captured military officers and their families were sent to Solikamski in Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: There she met Captain Anders Örbom I (1675-1740) who was also a prisoner and they married in September of 1719 and had their first child: Captain Anders Örbom II (1720-1783) in Russia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: They had the following children when they returned to Sweden: Carl Joachim Örbom, Captain (1721-1810) in the Swedish Army who married Beata Dorothea Von Saltza (1721-1764); Erik Johan Örbom, Major (1723-1802) of the Jämt
2 CONC land Regiment who married Helena Ruuth (1729-1802); Anna Catharina Örbom (1725-?); Gustaf Örbom I (1728-1730); Charlotta Örbom (1730-?); Gustaf Örbom II, Captain (1732-1807) who married Sophia Lovisa Winnberg (1744-1807); Christop
2 CONC her Örbom, Captain (1735-1828) who married E.M. Sundström (1736-?); Sara Elisabeth Örbom (1736-?); and Petrus Örbom, Lieutenant (1738-?).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Elisabeth von Rohr moved after her daughter Charlotta's death 1756 to Eastern part of the Empire, Egentliga Finland, Turku (Åbo)-area.  She was died in 1767 in the town of Naantali (Nådental) Humpis house owned by her daughte
2 CONC r's, Anna Catharina's husband Carl Bange.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relationship: Anna Elisabeth Von Rohr (1701-1744) was the sixth, great-grandmother of Richard Arthur Norton (1958- ).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Elisabet died September 7th in 1767 at Humpis in Nådendal (Naantali). She was buried September 9th in Nådendal (Naantali). Source: Naantali syntyneet-vihityt-kuolleet 1756-1809 (AP I C:3)  Sivu 34-35 K 1767 ; SSHY http://www.
2 CONC sukuhistoria.fi/sshy/sivut/jasenille/paikat.php?bid=19574&pnum=253 / Viitattu 02.11.2021
2 CONT
2 CONT Research by Wayne Ohlsson: Wayne Ohlsson writes on Memorial Day, May 25, 2020: "A distant cousin who is/was writing a book about Joakim von Rohr claims he was never in Poltava. Joachim von Rohr was major at Viborgs fort in the eas
2 CONC t of Finland. He lived there with his family. In about 1710 the Russians came and seiged the whole town of Viborg and the fort. They captured Joachim, his wife Anna Charlotta Klingenberg and their 4 children. Anna Elisabet, 18 yea
2 CONC rs was the oldest. The Russians took them all the family to Siberia. It was not easy for them and I have seen a letter in which Joachim himself tells that they got some help from ”the rich baron Struganof”.  [Anna Elisabeth Örbom 
2 CONC was buried at] Humpis, Nådendal, Åbo/Turku, Finland ... Her father could not have been at the Battle of Poltava when he was commander of the fort at Viborg"
2 CONT
2 CONT Research: Researched and written by Richard Arthur Norton (1958- ) for Findagrave starting on January  7, 2004. Updated on Memorial Day, May 25, 2020 with information from Wayne Ohlsson that corrects where she was taken prisoner f
2 CONC rom, it was Viborg and not Poltava.
2 CONT
2 CONT .Baltic. Her mother was Catharina Charlotta Klingenberg (1680-1758). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Prisoner of War: She was taken prisoner along with her father at the Battle of Poltava in the Ukraine. All captured military officers and their families were sent to Solikamski, Siberia in Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: There she met Anders Örbom I, Captain (1675-1740) who was also a prisoner and they married in September of 1719 and had their first child: Anders Örbom II, Captain (1720-1783) in Siberia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: They had the following children when they returned to Sweden: Carl Joachim Örbom, Captain (1721-1810) in the Swedish Army who married Beata Dorothea Von Saltza (1721-1764); Erik Johan Örbom, Major (1723-1802) of the Jämt
2 CONC land Regiment who married Helena Ruuth (1729-1802); Anna Catharina Örbom (1725-?); Gustaf Örbom I (1728-1730); Charlotta Örbom (1730-?); Gustaf Örbom II, Captain (1732-1807) who married Sophia Lovisa Winnberg (1744-1807); Christop
2 CONC her Örbom, Captain (1735-1828) who married E.M. Sundström (1736-?); Sara Elisabeth Örbom (1736-?); and Petrus Örbom, Lieutenant (1738-?).
2 CONT
2 CONT Relationship: Anna Elisabeth Von Rohr (1701-1744) was the sixth, great-grandmother of Richard Arthur Norton (1958- ). 
