2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia 
2 CONT 9000 B.C. Around the time the last Ice Age ended allowing people to settle in the Isle of Mull
2 CONT   500 A.D. Irish migrants invaded Mull and the surrounding coast and established the Gaelic kingdom of Dál Riata.
2 CONT   500 A.D. The kingdom was divided into a number of regions, each controlled by a different kinship group. One of these, the Cenél Loairn, controlled Mull and the adjacent mainland to the east.
2 CONT   563 A.D. Christianity arrives to the Isle of Mull via the island of Iona when St. Columba arrives
2 CONT   800 A.D. Vikings invaded and destroy Dál Riata and replace it with the Norse Kingdom of the Isles 
2 CONT   872 A.D. Norwegian unification happens causing the land to become a part of the Kingdom of Norway 
2 CONT   872 A.D.  The island kingdom became known as Suðreyjar which means southern isles in Old Norse
2 CONT 1098 A.D.  The King of Scotland tries to reinstate the Scottish rite of land and kick out Magnus Barefoot
2 CONT 1160 A.D. A coup caused by a Norse-Gael named Somerled claims the Suðreyjar and creates an ind. Kingdom
2 CONT 1164 A.D. Somerled dies, and the realm is divided by the Crovan Dynasty (his brother in law) and children
2 CONT 1164 A.D.  His Son Dougall receives the former territory of the Cenél Loairn, now known as Lorn, where Mull is
2 CONT 1237 A.D. Kings of the Hebrides control the Sound of Mull they were the heirs of Dougall --> MacDougall's 
2 CONT 1500 A.D. The MacLean's regain control of the Isle of Mull after the MacDonalds were exiled, they were the successors of the Cenél Loairn kinship, and their land was restored by James IV, King of Scotland
2 CONT 1681 A.D. The Campbells seize the isle of Mull the MacLeans move to Cairnburgh castle in the treshnish islands
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Castles under the control of the MacLeans at one point or another
2 CONT Duart Castle
2 CONT Moy Castle
2 CONT Cairnburgh Castle
2 CONT Torosay Castle
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In the late 11th century, Magnus Barefoot, the Norwegian king, launched a military campaign which in 1098 led the king of Scotland to quitclaim to Magnus all claim of sovereign authority over the territory of the Kingdom of the Is
2 CONC les. However, a coup some 60 years later, led by a Norse-Gael named Somerled, detached the whole of the Suðreyjar from Norway and transformed it into an independent kingdom. After Somerled's death in 1164, nominal Norwegian author
2 CONC ity was established, but practical control of the realm was divided between Somerled's sons and the heirs of Somerled's brother-in-law, the Crovan Dynasty. His son Dougall received the former territory of the Cenél Loairn, now kno
2 CONC wn as Lorn, of which Mull formed part.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
