1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  GORDON Viscount KENMURE.
2 CONT
2 CONT The peerage of Scotland: containing an historical and genealogical account of the nobility of that kingdom, ... collected from the public records, and ancient chartularies of this nation, ... Illustrated with copper-plates. By Rob
2 CONC ert Douglas, Esq;. Douglas, Robert, Sir, 1694-1770  Page  367-373/4
2 CONT
2 CONT AS the origin, grandeur, and antiquity of the great and illustrious sirname of Gordon is fully set forth under the title of duke of Gordon, we shall proceed to deduce the descent of this noble family from their immediate ancestor,
2 CONC  viz.
2 CONT
2 CONT VI. Sir ADAM de GORDON, head or chief of the family of Gordon, was the sixth generation of that illustrious house from Richard, who lived in the reigns of king Malcolm IV. and king William the Lion, and the first who settled in th
2 CONC e north of Scotland. He was one of the greatest men of his time, was killed at the battle of Halidonhill, anno 1333, and lest issue four sons.
2 CONT * 1. Sir Alexander, who succeeded his father in all his lands in the north. Vide title duke of Gordon.
2 CONT * 2. William de Gordon, the first of this family.
2 CONT * 3. John de Gordon.
2 CONT * 4. Thomas de Gordon.
2 CONT
2 CONT VII.  WILLIAM de GORDON , second son of sir Adam, got from his father most of his lands in the south of Scotland, viz. the lands and barony of Stitchel, &c. which appears from a charter of Thomas Randolf earl of Murray, to sir Ada
2 CONC m de Gordon knight, and William Gordon his son, and his heirs, of the lands and tenement of Stitchel, with the pertinents, &c. which charter is confirmed by king Robert Bruce, anno 1315.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also the lands of Glenkenns in Galloway, which were acquired by sir Adam Gordon from John de Maxwell, anno 1297, and which comprehended the lands of Lochinvar, Kenmure, &c. whereby he became one of the most considerable barons of 
2 CONC that country.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the battle of Durham, in 1346, it seems he had been guilty of some unwarrantable practices with his neighbours the borderers, which appears from an ample remission from William lord Douglas, then guardian of Scotland, to Wil
2 CONC liam de Gordon, and all his followers from Galloway, whereby they are received into the faith and peace of their sovereign lord the king, and are restored to all their heritages and conquests every where within the kingdom, and di
2 CONC scharges them of all transgressions they had committed after the battle of Durham,&c. The remission is dated the 9th May 1354.
2 CONT
2 CONT He obtained also from Robert earl of Strathearn, (afterwards king Robert II.) a charter, Willielmo Gordon domino de Stitchel, of the heretable keeping of the new forest of Glenkenns in Galloway, in as ample a manner as the said ea
2 CONC rl had got it himself from David king of Scotland, his uncle, &c. The grant is dated 8th April 1358.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died about the year 1370, and was succeeded by his son,
2 CONT
2 CONT VIII. ROGER de GORDON of Stitchel, who, with sir William Borthwick, were appointed commissioners to a treaty with the English about settling the marches, which they happily concluded. The agreement bearing date at Clochmabanestane
2 CONC  the 6th November 1398.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed in the service of his country at the battle of Homildon, together with his chief sir Adam, and many of his brave countrymen, anno 1402, and was succeeded by his son,
2 CONT
2 CONT IX. Sir ALEXANDER GORDON of Stitchel, who was retoured heir to his father, and infest in the lands of Kenmure on a precept of sasine, obtained from Archibald earl of Douglas, then superior of Galloway, dated 24th January 1403.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earl of Douglas, having been long a prisoner in England, obtained liberty to come to Scotland, and sir Alexander Gordon became one of the hostages for his ransom, or return into England, or for his surrendering himself a true 
2 CONC prisoner to John of Lancaster, the king's son, or to the king himself; and gets letters of safe conduct for going into England, entering himself hostage, &c. anno 1408.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earl afterwards, in consideration of these good services, and on payment of fiftyfive nobles in gold, gives him a ratification of the rights of all his lands in the lordship of Galloway; also a new grant, dated 28th May 1408, 
2 CONC of the twenty-merk land of old extent, of Balmaclellan, lying in the lordship of Galloway, which is still in the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT And, by another deed dated in June 1412, he is made bailie of the barony of Earlstoun,&c.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left issue two sons.
2 CONT * 1. Roger de Gordon.
2 CONT * 2. Adam of Holm, whose son Quintin de Gordon was infeft in the lands of Holm in 1465, of whom William Gordon late of Holm, now of Craig, is the lineal heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT X. ROGER de GORDON, who by a charter, as proprietor of the lands and barony of Stitchel, confirms to St. Mary, and the monks of Jedburgh, a grant formerly made to them, of two ox-gangs of land by William de Gordon, sometime lord o
2 CONC f Stitchel, dated 1st June 1431.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1439, he made a resignation of his lands of Stitchel in favours of his son William, reserving to himself the liferent.
2 CONT
2 CONT And dying about the year 1442, was succeeded by his son the said,
2 CONT
2 CONT XI. [https://www.geni.com/people/William-Gordon-of-Stitchill-1st-of-Lochinvar/6000000007604334848 WILLIAM de GORDON], promiscuously designed of Stitchel and Lochinvar; which last was part of the lands in Galloway, acquired by sir 
2 CONC Adam Gordon from John de Maxwell in 1297, before noticed, and which continued to be the chief title of the family till they were nobilitate, and this William was the first of them that settled and resided in Galloway.
2 CONT
2 CONT He got from king James II. a charter of the lands and barony of Stitchel, Willielmo de Gordon, filio et haeredi apparenti Rogeri de Gordon, domini de Stitchel,&c. dated 7th February 1440.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married—, by whom he had four sons and one daughter.
2 CONT * 1. John, his heir.
2 CONT * 2. Alexander, who, in 1490, purchased the lands of Auchinreoch, &c. and was ancestor of the Gordons of Aird, now Earlstoun, and others.
2 CONT * 3. George, who acquired the lands of Troquhain in 1494, and of him the present George Gordon of Troquhain is lineally descended.
2 CONT * 4. Roger, who married—MacNaught, heiress of Crago, and was ancestor of the present Alexander Gordon of Crago.
2 CONT * His daughter, Margaret, married to sir Thomas MacLellan of Bombie, ancestor of lord Kirkcudbright.
2 CONT
2 CONT He survived the year 1450, and was succeeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT XII. [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-John-Gordon-Of-Lochinvar-Knight/6000000027466055394 Sir JOHN GORDON of Lochinvar], who got a charter from king James IV. dilecto armigero nostro Johanni Gordon de Lochinvar,totas et integras q
2 CONC uadraginta solidatas terrarum de Corscranthane antiqui extentus, &c. dated in 1492.
2 CONT
2 CONT And another charter of the lands of Middlethird of Kirkcormack,&c. dated in 1501.
2 CONT
2 CONT He got a grant of the bailiary of Kirkanders from Patrick earl of Bothwell, anno 1504.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also three charters from king James IV. of a great many other lands in 1506, 1507, and 1509; by all which it appears he was possessed of a vast estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 1st, Annabella, daughter of Robert lord Boyd, by Marian, daughter of sir Robert Maxwell of Calderwood, by whom he had a son, 
2 CONT * Alexander de Gordon.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 2dly, Elizabeth Lindsay, by whom he had three sons and two daughters.
2 CONT * 1. Sir Robert, of Accarsan of Glenn, who carried on the line of this family, of whom afterwards.
2 CONT * 2. William, ancestor of the Gordons of Crauchlaw, of whom are descended the Gordons of Pulvenan, Grange, and Balmeg, &c. in vicecomitatu de Wigton.
2 CONT * 3. John, who obtained from his father the lands of Balmaclellan, now Hardlands, purchased from the daughters of Quintin Gordon, a son of the family of Holm, infeft in these lands in 1465, as before observed.
2 CONT * 1st daughter, Elizabeth, married to sir William Douglas of Drumlanrig, ancestor of the duke of Queensberry.
2 CONT * 2. Janet, married, 1st, to Alexander Stewart of Gairlies, ancestor of the earl of Galloway; and, 2dly, to sir William Keith of Inverugie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John had also a natural son, to whom he gave the lands of Crathlet, upon which he got a charter, wherein he is designed Willielmus filius domini Johannis Gordon de Lochinvar, dated in 1506. He was afterwards legitimate on 23d 
2 CONC March 1538.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John died in the end of 1512, and was succeeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT XIII. Sir ALEXANDER de GORDON, who, in his father's lifetime, was designed of Kenmure.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a man of fine parts, and in great favour with king James III. who made him one of the gentlemen of his bed-chamber when a young man, and gave him a new grant of the superiority of the lands of Kenmure, Lagan, Balmaclellan, 
2 CONC &c. the superiority being then in the crown, by the forfeiture of the earl of Douglas, and erected them all into one free barony, holding blench of the crown; upon which he got a charter, Alexandro Gordon de Kenmure, filio et haer
2 CONC edi apparenti Johannis Gordon de Lochinvar, &c. dated anno 1487, which was afterwards confirmed by king James IV. anno 1489.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some time thereafter he had the misfortune to kill John Dunbar of Mochrum, then steward of Kirkcudbright, for which he was obliged to abscond, and his father and friends were exeemed from the jurisdiction of the said steward, on a
2 CONC ccount of the deadly feud subsisting for the said slaughter, by a privy seal, dated 4th September 1508.
2 CONT
2 CONT He afterwards got a charter from king James IV. of the lands and barony of Stitchel, &c. Alexandro de Gordon, militi, filio et haeredi apparenti Johannis Gordon de Lochinvar,et Elizahethae Stewart sponsae suae, &c. dated in 1512.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 1st, Janet, daughter of sir William Douglas of Drumlanrig and Elizabeth Crichton his spouse, by a dispensation from the Pope, on account of consanguinity, but she died without issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 2dly, Elizabeth Stewart, sister to James earl of Murray, by whom he had one daughter.
2 CONT *Jean Gordon, who claimed his estate, but after a long process before the lords of council, she was obliged to renounce all right she had to the estate of Kenmure, &c. in favours of her uncle sir Robert,anno 1517. To which deed th
2 CONC e lords of council are witnesses.   She married Lauchlan Maclntosh of that ilk, who, in consideration of her being an heiress, quartered her arms with his own, and she, with consent of her said husband, ratified her former renunci
2 CONC ation, anno 1520.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Alexander had also a natural son, named Roger, who was legitimate, anno 1546.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed with his royal leader king James IV. at the battle of Floudon, in 1513, and was succeeded by his brother,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Gordon/6000000000748079340 XIII. Sir ROBERT GORDON], formerly designed of Accarsan of Glen, now of Lochinvar, who got a charter from king James V. Roberto de Gordon, militi, of the lands of Kenm
2 CONC ure, Lagan, &c. dated in 1517.
2 CONT
2 CONT He obtained from queen Mary a grant of the clerkships of the sheriffdom of Wigton, and stewarty of Kirkcudbright during life, with power to officiate by deputes.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Marian, daughter and sole heiress of John Accarsan of Glenshyreburn, now called Rusco, by whom he got all these lands, and built the house of Rusco, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT By her he had issue six sons, and three daughters.
2 CONT * 1. Sir James.
2 CONT * 2. John, who, in 1518, got a charter of the lands of Barnbarrock, and Barnhausie, and was ancestor of the Gordons of Haslefield.
2 CONT * 3. Alexander, who acquired the lands of Gaitgill, anno 1517; also the lands of Darmolane, and Markbane, in vicecom. de Wigton.
2 CONT * 4. George.
2 CONT * 5. Roger.
2 CONT * 6. David, who married Isabel, daughter of John Muirhead of Culr•och, by whom he got the lands of Carstramine, &c.
2 CONT * 1st daughter, Catharine, married to Patrick Agnew of Salquhary, ancestor of sir Andrew Agnew of Lochnaw.
2 CONT * 2. Elizabeth, married, 1st, to Uthred MacDowal of Machermore; 2dly, to Alexander Livingston of Little-Airds.
2 CONT * 3. Janet, married to James, son and heir of Alexander Ogilvie of that ilk, ancestor of the earl of Finlater.
2 CONT
2 CONT And dying about 1520, was succeeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/James-Gordon-of-Lochinvar/6000000006322753199 XIV. Sir JAMES GORDON] of Lochinvar, a man of great accomplishments, and highly esteemed by king James V. from whom he got a charter, Jacobo Gordon de Loch
2 CONC invar, militi, of the lands of Hardlands, Minebog, and others, dated in 1539.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also a charter,duarum mercat. cum dimidia mercat. &c. jacen. in baronia de Balmage, &c. dated in 1541.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was made the king's chamberlain for five years of the lordship of Galloway, by a writ dated 10th March 1528: and by another, dated the 1st April 1537, he is made governor of the town, castle, and sortalice of Douglas, and chamb
2 CONC erlain of that lordship, then in the crown by forseiture.
2 CONT
2 CONT This sir James Gordon, with sir James Douglas of Drumlanrig, and thirty-seven others, got a remission for the slaughter of Thomas MacLellan of Bombie, committed in the city of Edinburgh, dated in 1529.
2 CONT
2 CONT And being in great favour with the king, he was one of those chosen to accompany him when he went to bring home his queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James, upon that occasion, obtained a writ from the king, taking all his friends and followers under the immediate care of the government, and freeing them from answering to courts for any misdemeanor whatsomever, till sir Jam
2 CONC es's return to Scotland; and as his friends and followers are all named in the writ of privy seal, it sufficiently shows what a numerous, flourishing, and considerable family that of Lochinvar then was.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Margaret, daughter and sole heiress of Robert Crichton of Kirkpatrick, nephew of sir Robert Crichton of Sanquhar, ancestor of the earl of Dumsries, by whom he got many lands in the parish of Glencairn, and by her had fi
2 CONC ve sons, and five daughters.
2 CONT * 1. John, his heir.
2 CONT * 2. William Gordon of Pennygame, ancestor of the present lord Kenmure, of whom afterwards.
2 CONT * 3. Robert, who got a grant of the lands of Muirfad, 21st July 1544; but dying unmarried, his estate went to his nephew John, son of his brother William of Pennygame.
2 CONT * 4. James, who got possession of the lands of Hardlands, by a charter, dated 2d June 1540.
2 CONT * 5. Alexander, who got a tack, from the crown, of the lands of Slagnaw in Kelton, and married Janet Kennedy, relict of John Kennedy of Largs.
2 CONT * 1st daughter, Janet, married, 1st, to William earl of Glencairn; and, 2dly, to Patrick Agnew, sheri•• of Wigton.
2 CONT * 2. Margaret, married to William, son and heir of sir James Douglas of Drumlanrig, ancestor of the duke of Queensberry.
2 CONT * 3. Catharine, married to sir James MacCulloch of Cardness.
2 CONT * 4. Helen, married to sir Thomas MacLellan of Bombie, ancestor of lord Kirkcudbright.
2 CONT * 5. Elizabeth, married, 1st, to William Grierson of Lag; 2dly, to William Adair of Kenhit
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James was killed at the battle of Pinkie, in 1547, and was succeeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-John-Gordon-of-Lochinvar/6000000006885224163 XV. Sir JOHN GORDON] of Lochinvar, a man of great honour, loyalty, and integrity, who suffered greatly for his firm adherence to the interest of queen M
2 CONC ary. In 1555, the queen appointed him justiciar of the stewarty of Galloway; and her son, king James, renewed his commission anno 1587.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1561, he entered into a contract with the predecessors of the duke of Queensberry, earl of Dumfries, sir Robert Kilpatrick, sir William Grierson, &c. whereby they were bound to stand by one another against all mortals,
2 CONC  to keep together in all assemblies, armies, and wars, and to submit all differences amongst themselves to the majority, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1567, he is one of the subscribers of the bond for confirming the king's authority, and securing the government, as established by law.
2 CONT
2 CONT He obtained a charter from queen Mary, of a great many lands,Johanni Gordon de Lochinvar, militi, &c. dated anno 1565.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also six charters from king James VI. domino Johanni Gordon de Lochinvar, militi, of several other lands; and particularly one, Johanni Gordon,militi, filio et haeredi Margaretae Crichton, filiae et haeredis quondam Roberti Cricht
2 CONC on de Kirkpatrick, &c. totas et integras terras de, &c. dated in 1580.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1562, he made a resignation of his whole estate in favours of his brother, William Gordon of Pennygame, failing heirs male of his own body, he having then no maleissue; and this William's grand-son's grandson actually succeeded
2 CONC  to the honours of Kenmure, as will be shown hereafter.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 1st, Juliana, daughter of— Home of Wedderburn, by whom he had one daughter,
2 CONT * Margaret, married to Hugh earl of Loudoun, in 1572.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1563, he married, 2dly, dame Elizabeth Maxwell, daughter of sir John Maxwell of Terreagles, afterwards lord Herries in right of his mother, by whom he had five sons, and four daughters.
2 CONT * 1. Sir Robert, designed in his father's lifetime sir Robert of Glen.
2 CONT * 2. William, appointed commendator of Glenluce in 1581, and infeft in the lands of Glenquicken and Garrocher in 1588; but died without issue.
2 CONT * 3. John Gordon of Buitle, who died also without issue.
2 CONT * 4. James of Barncrosh, afterwards of Buitle, whose son John succeeded to the estate and honours of Kenmure, as hereafter.
2 CONT * 5. Alexander Gordon of Enrig.
2 CONT * 1st daughter, Mary, married to Alexander Kennedy of Bargeny.
2 CONT * 2. Janet, married to John MacDowal of Garthland.
2 CONT * 3. Elizabeth, married to Alexander Stewart of Gairlies, ancestor of the earl of Galloway.
2 CONT * 4. Grizel, married to James lord Carlisse of Torthorald.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had also a natural son William, infeft in the Kirkland of Balmaclellan in 1570, and legitimate in 1574.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John dying in 1604, was succeeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Robert-Gordon-of-Lochinvar/6000000006872959664  XVI. Sir ROBERT GORDON], formerly of Glen, now of Lochinvar, who, in his younger days, was one of the strongest and most active men of his time.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the three defendants at the famous tournament, proclaimed by king James VI. to whom his daughter, princess Elizabeth, delivered the prizes, though her own brother prince Henry, and several others of the first rank, w
2 CONC ere challengers.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also gave many remarkable instances of his strength and courage, in defence of Galloway against the inhabitants of Annandale, whose cattle, having been carried off by the English, they were in use of making reprisals on their n
2 CONC ext neighbours.
2 CONT
2 CONT In one of those encounters, they having killed James Gordon of Lochinkitt, his friend and follower, he went down the country, and burnt the houses of Gratney, Wamphrey, Lockerby, Reidhall, Langrigs, &c. and killed Richard Irvine o
2 CONC f Gratney, and took several others of them prisoners, for which, and some other daring actions of that kind, king James sent out a strong party to apprehend him; but he audaciously deforced the king's party, laid violent hands on 
2 CONC the officer who commanded them, and made him eat and swallow his majesty's warrant for apprehending him.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, being at last weary of these lawless practices, his father and friends made intercession with the king, and obtained from him an ample remission for all his former misbehaviour, upon which he came to court, and soon becam
2 CONC e so great a favourite of the king, that he made him one of the gentlemen of his bed-chamber.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his accession to the crown of England, he bestowed upon him several lands, then in the crown by the annexation in 1587, by which he became possessed of a vast estate, as appears from many charters, domino Roberto Gordon de L
2 CONC ochinvar, militi, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1621, he was created a baronet, by a charter,Roberto Gordon de Lochinvar, militi, baroniae de Galloway in America, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married lady Elizabeth Ruthven, eldest daughter of John earl of Gowrie, by whom he had two sons, and two daughters.
2 CONT * 1. Sir John of Lochinvar, afterwards viscount Kenmure.
2 CONT * 2. Robert of Gilston, who got a grant from the crown of the barony of Galloway, in Nova Scotia, with many ample privileges; but he died without issue.
2 CONT * 1st daughter; Elizabeth, married to John lord Herries, afterwards earl of Nithsdale.
2 CONT * 2. Isabel, married to Alexander Fraser, younger of Philorth, ancestor of lord Salton.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had also a natural son, John, who was infeft in the lands of Haslefield,anno 1624, and married Elizabeth, only daughter and heiress of Alexander Gordon of Carstramon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert dying in 1628, was succeeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Gordon/6000000006864142550 XVII. Sir JOHN GORDON of Lochinvar], who, in his father's lifetime, got a charter of the •ive pound land of Nether-Barcapel, the five merk land of Kirkonnel, Blackmark,&
2 CONC c. Johanni de Gordon filio legitimo natu maximo, domini Roberti Gordon de Lochinvar, &c. dated anno 1619.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a great loyalist, and steady friend of king Charles I. who highly esteemed him, and raised him to the dignity of the peerage, by the titles of viscount Kenmure, lord Lochinvar, &c. by letters patent, dated 8th May 1633, to 
2 CONC him et haeredibus masculis quibuscunque, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT And as a further testimony of his majesty's favour, he got part of his lands erected into a royal burrow, with ample jurisdiction, to be called the burgh of Galloway, now New-Galloway.
2 CONT
2 CONT This sir John sold his barony of Stitchel, which had been so long in the possession of his family; and, it is said, gave the price of it in a purse to the duke of Buckingham, in hopes that he would favour his title to the earldom 
2 CONC of Gowrie, which he claimed in right of his mother, eldest daughter of John the last earl: but, this is said to have happened the very night before the duke was stabbed by Felton, and so had no effect.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married lady Jean Campbell, daughter of Archibald seventh earl of Argyle, by whom he had a son and successor,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Gordon/6000000039679361861 XVIII. JOHN, second viscount Kenmure], to whom Archibald lord Lo•n, afterwards marquis of Argyle, and William earl of Morton, were tutors testamentar; but he dying witho
2 CONC ut issue, was succeeded in his estate and honours by his cousin and heir-male John, son of James of Barn•ro••, to whom we now return.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/James-Gordon-of-Buittle/6000000014986859553 XVI. JAMES GORDON of Barnerosh and Buitle], a younger son of sir John Gordon of Lochinvar, by Elizabeth, daughter of sir John Maxwell of Terreagles, who marr
2 CONC ied Margaret, daughter of sir John Vaus, and relict of John Glendoning of Drumrash, by whom he had two sons.
2 CONT * 1. John.
2 CONT * 2. Robert.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/James-Gordon/6000000018575883156 XVII. JOHN] the eldest son succeeded to the estate and honours of Kenmure, as above, and was the third viscount Kenmure; but he dying without issue, was succeeded by hi
2 CONC s brother,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Gordon/6000000018575253583 XVII. ROBERT, fourth viscount Kenmure], a man of great loyalty and integrity, who suffered many hardships on account of his firm attachment to the royal family. His es
2 CONC tate was forfeited by the parliament, and a reward put upon his head by Oliver Cromwell, to any who should bring him in dead or alive.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, he survived the restoration, went to court, and married one of the ladies of the bedchamber,anno 1661. He returned to Scotland immediately thereafter, and died at Greenlaw that same year without issue, whereby his estate 
2 CONC and honours devolved upon his next heir-male, John of Pennygame, lineally descended of the forementioned William Gordon of Pennygame, to whom we now return.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/William-Gordon/6000000018573588971 XV. WILLIAM GORDON of Pennygame], second son of sir [https://www.geni.com/people/James-Gordon-of-Lochinvar/6000000006322753199 James Gordon of Lochinvar, [No. XIV. of
2 CONC  these memoirs,] by Margaret his wife, daughter and heiress of Robert Crichton of Kirkpatrick], was infeft in the lands of Kilreoch and Grobdale, by a charter dated anno 1542.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Helen, daughter of Alexander Stewart of Gairlies, ancestor of the earl of Galloway, by whom he had a son and successor,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Gordon-2nd-of-Pennynghame/6000000018573339957  XVI. JOHN GORDON] of Pennygame, who succeeded also to the lands of Muirfad, upon the death of his uncle Robert, as before observed.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his son,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-GORDON-of-Penninghame/6000000014092772686 XVII. ALEXANDER GORDON] of Pennygame, who was a great loyalist, but died about 1645, having contracted a great deal of debt in the beginning of the c
2 CONC ivil commotions. This appears by several apprisings and adjudications led against his estate after his death, of which we shall only mention one,viz. appris•• at the instance of—Ferguson of Craigdarroch against the lands of Pennyg
2 CONC ame and others, which belonged to the deceast Alexander Gordon of Pennygame, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left issue a son and successor,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/William-GORDON-of-Penninghame/6000000014092852438 XVIII. WILLIAM GORDON] of Pennygame, who married—daughter of—, by whom he had issue two sons.
2 CONT * 1. John, afterwards viscount Kenmure.
2 CONT * 2. Alexander, who carried on the line of this family.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died about 1660, and was succeeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Gordon/6000000014092658943 XIX. JOHN GORDON of Pennygame], who, upon the death of Robert fourth viscount Kenmure, succeeded to his estate and honours as before observed, anno 1661, and was the fif
2 CONC th viscount, but dying without issue in 1662, was succeeded by his brother,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-Gordon-5th-Viscount-Kenmure/6000000003423629680 XIX. ALEXANDER, sixth viscount Kenmure], who, upon his brother's succession to the honours of Kenmure, was designed by the title of Pennygame, 
2 CONC which appears by an inhibition,
2 CONT
2 CONT "at the instance of John Scott merchant burgess of Edinburgh, against Alexander Gordon of Pennygame, now viscount Kenmure, heir-male to umquhil Robert viscount Kenmure his predecessor, who was heir-male to umquhill John viscount K
2 CONC enmure his predecessor, who was heir-male to umquhil John viscount Kenmure his predecessor, who was son and heir to umquhil John, first viscount Kenmure, his father, then designed John Gordon of Lochinvar, &c. and sua heir-male by
2 CONC  progress, to him upon the passive titles, &c."
2 CONT
2 CONT dated 6th July 1663. He afterwards got a charter under the great seal, of the lands of Kenmure, &c. dated anno 1676.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his younger years he was bred to the army, came early into the revolution, and had the command of a regiment at the battle of Killycrankie, where many of his officers, and most of his men were slain.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 1st, the daughter and heiress of—Gordon of Auchlauin, by whom he had one daughter,
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes, married, 1st, to William Maxwell of Kelton, second son of the earl of Nithsdale; 2dly, to John Lindsay of Wauchop.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 2dly, Marian, daughter of —MacCulloch of Ardwell, by whom he had a son,
2 CONT * William, his heir,—and three daughters,
2 CONT * 1. Jean, married to William Gordon of Skirmers.
2 CONT * 2. Marian, married to sir Alexander Gordon of Earlstoun.
2 CONT * 3. Elizabeth, married, 1st, to Samuel Maxwell of Newlaw; 2dly, to Samuel Brown of Mollance.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 3dly, lady Grizel Stewart, daughter of James earl of Galloway, by whom he had two sons and three daughters.
2 CONT * 1. John Gordon of Greenlaw, who married Nicholas, daughter of—Stewart of Castlestewart, and had issue.
2 CONT * 2. James Gordon, Esq; who married Grizel, eldest daughter and heiress of William Gordon of Grange.
2 CONT * 1st daughter, Mary, married to sir Patrick Maxwell of Springkell, Bart.
2 CONT * 2. Grizel, married to Mr. Robert Gordon of Dundeugh.
2 CONT * 3. Isabel, married to John MacGhie of Balmaghie.
2 CONT
2 CONT He dying in 1698, was succeeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/William-Gordon-6th-Viscount-Kenmure-Lord-Lochinvar/6000000003423454933 XX. WILLIAM, seventh viscount Kenmure], who married Mary, daughter of sir John Dalziel of Glenae, and sister of Robert earl of Car
2 CONC nwath, by whom he had three sons, and one daughter.
2 CONT * 1. Robert, his heir.
2 CONT * 2. John, who succeeded his brother.
2 CONT * 3. James, died unmarried.
2 CONT * His daughter Henriet, married her mother's cousin-german, John Dalziel, Esq; son of captain James Dalziel, brother to sir John Dalziel of Glenae foresaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT This viscount had the misfortune to be engaged in the rebellion 1715, was taken prisoner at Preston, tried by his peers, condemned and executed, and his estate and honours were forfeited to the crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Gordon/6000000014092902203 XXI. ROBERT], his eldest son, had it not been for the forfeiture, would have been eighth viscount Kenmure; but he dying unmarried, was succeeded by his brother,
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Gordon/6000000003423732010 XXI. JOHN], who now represents that noble family, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was bred to the army, but is now retired to his seat in the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married lady Frances MacKenzie, daughter of William earl of Seasorth, by whom he hath issue four sons and one daughter.
2 CONT * 1. William.
2 CONT * 2. John.
2 CONT * 3. Adam.
2 CONT * 4. Robert.
2 CONT * The daughter, Frances.
2 CONT
2 CONT ARMS.
2 CONT
2 CONT Azure, three boars heads erazed or.
2 CONT
2 CONT CREST; on a wreath, a demi-savage, proper, wreathed about his temples and middle with laurel.
2 CONT
2 CONT SUPPORTERS; two savages wreathed as the crest, each holding in his outer hand a battoon erect, proper.
2 CONT
2 CONT MOTTO; Dread God.
2 CONT
2 CONT CHIEF SEATS. At Kenmure-castle, &c. in the stewarty of Kirckudbright
